-- chem/pairone.cub — the two-electron ion at charge Z, both ends from one function. H = H₀ + V, H₀ the two electrons
-- without repulsion, V = 1/r₁₂ > 0, V⁻¹ = r₁₂ a multiplication. For any φ, Cauchy–Schwarz on V^{1/2}ψ and V^{−1/2}φ gives
-- ⟨ψ,φ⟩² ≤ ⟨ψ,Vψ⟩⟨φ,V⁻¹φ⟩, that is V ≥ |φ⟩⟨φ|/⟨φ,V⁻¹φ⟩. With φ₀ = e^{−Z(r₁+r₂)} (H₀'s ground state, level −Z²):
-- H ≥ H₀ + |φ₀⟩⟨φ₀|/⟨r₁₂⟩, diagonal in H₀'s states, its level on φ₀ being −Z² + 1/⟨r₁₂⟩ and elsewhere H₀'s (≥ −5Z²/8, the
-- 1s2s level); and ⟨φ₀,Hφ₀⟩ = −Z² + ⟨1/r₁₂⟩ is the Ritz upper end. The integrals are chem/he.cub's J at α = Z:
-- ⟨r₁₂^c⟩ = J(1,1,c+1)/J(1,1,1), each J a constant times a power of 1/Z, so ⟨r₁₂⟩ = 35/(16Z) (`pr-r`) and ⟨1/r₁₂⟩ = 5Z/8
-- (`pr-v`) are identities between constant multiples of Z⁻⁶ and Z⁻⁵, proved by one point, checked at four. The 2×2 Gram
-- determinant of V^{1/2}φ₀ and V^{−1/2}φ₀ is ⟨V⟩⟨V⁻¹⟩ − 1 = 47/128 (`pr-cs`), which is what orders the two ends: upper −
-- lower = 47Z/280 (`pr-gap`). The lower end is the level on φ₀ when it lies below H₀'s next level: −5Z²/8 − (−Z² + 16Z/35)
-- = Z(105Z − 128)/280 (`pr-below`), nonnegative for Z ≥ 128/105 by its form. So for Z ≥ 128/105 the ground energy lies in
-- [−Z² + 16Z/35, −Z² + 5Z/8]; helium in [−108/35, −11/4] (`pr-he`). The polynomial identities have degree at most 2 in
-- Z, so the four points of `pr-grid` prove them. Taken here, not proved: the operator inequality V ≥ |φ⟩⟨φ|/⟨φ,V⁻¹φ⟩ on
-- the function class (Cauchy–Schwarz there), H₀'s spectrum (levels −Z²/2·(1/n² + 1/m²), φ₀ its ground state), and
-- Ritz's comparison for the upper end.
